#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110289 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110701 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfadce43-b527-59fc-b6c7-ec963bb543b4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Inverses of Exponential Functions With More Than Two Transformations"}] :question/id #uuid "09634460-21c4-5389-92f4-15339282b7ff" :question/math-academy-id "e-10455" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x) = \\dfrac12 e^{x+8}-2.$" :question/worked-solution "To find $f^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x+8}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{y+8}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{y+8}-2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{y+8} & =x+2 \\\\ {e}^{y+8} & =2x+4 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{y+8}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4) \\\\ y+8 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4) \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4)-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n     \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is $f^{-1}(x) = \\ln{(2x+4)} - 8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110500 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110702 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2efb7d6d-25e9-5bf3-b2b3-5085221b0e76" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Inverses of Logarithmic Functions With Up to Two Transformations"}] :question/id #uuid "fc54368a-099e-5b85-a6c4-15db9be3ee8f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4127" :question/problem "What is the inverse of $f(x) =\\ln(x+3)+6?$" :question/worked-solution "To find $f^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\ny = \\ln(x+3)+6\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+3)+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+3)+6 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+3) & =x-6 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+3)} & ={e}^{x-6} \\\\ y+3 & ={e}^{x-6} \\\\ y & ={e}^{x-6}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is $f^{-1}(x) = e^{x-6}-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110501 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110700 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c01b24c5-c253-5a11-8055-9be0d60da592" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Inverses of Exponential Functions With Up to Two Transformations"}] :question/id #uuid "2b08436f-c561-59c5-bb79-621a1780b470" :question/math-academy-id "e-4128" :question/problem "The function $f(x) = e^{x-k}+2$ has an inverse function given by $f^{-1}(x) = \\ln{(x-2)}+4.$ What is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To find $f^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{x-k}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={e}^{y-k}+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={e}^{y-k}+2 \\\\ {e}^{y-k} & =x-2 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{y-k}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2) \\\\ y-k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2) \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is $f^{-1}(x) = \\ln{(x-2)} + k.$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110502 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110703 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a220ac5f-7b5c-59ef-82d5-aa84654f9fc2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Inverses of Logarithmic Functions With More Than Two Transformations"}] :question/id #uuid "c6d0d3ad-111a-5db1-b521-0c9416dfb6b8" :question/math-academy-id "e-4140" :question/problem "What is the inverse of $f(x) = 2\\log_4(3x)-1?$" :question/worked-solution "To find $f^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2{log}_{4}⁡(3x)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2{log}_{4}⁡(3y)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2{log}_{4}⁡(3y)-1 \\\\ 2{log}_{4}⁡(3y) & =x+1 \\\\ {log}_{4}⁡(3y) & =\\frac{x+1}{2} \\\\ {4}^{{log}_{4}⁡(3y)} & ={4}^{(x+1)/2} \\\\ 3y & ={4}^{(x+1)/2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}{4}^{(x+1)/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is $f^{-1}(x) =\\dfrac13 4^{(x+1)/2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537918 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110700 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c01b24c5-c253-5a11-8055-9be0d60da592" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Inverses of Exponential Functions With Up to Two Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "500968d9-0b02-594f-9796-38a6d0857002" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537395} {:answer/id #uuid "64a8ae10-ee67-50d8-90ac-7e8244b9414e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537430} {:answer/id #uuid "b0712310-2288-594f-a713-0325d1c58202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537566} {:answer/id #uuid "beb19ed9-fffa-5b00-9533-3a872c9ee6ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537598} {:answer/id #uuid "ce4ef968-3737-5e23-9278-d9b7973ad60c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537633}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0712310-2288-594f-a713-0325d1c58202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537566} :answer-field/id #uuid "b91ce61b-a6cb-5ce7-8bdc-e10fbcf3ddfa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6181c174-0cfd-5153-a1f3-884abcf7f2a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-148278" :question/problem "The function $f(x)={e}^{x+k}+3$ has an inverse function given by ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)+5.$ What is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{x+k}+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={e}^{y+k}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={e}^{y+k}+3 \\\\ {e}^{y+k} & =x-3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{y+k}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3) \\\\ y+k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3) \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)-k.$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=-5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537946 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110700 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c01b24c5-c253-5a11-8055-9be0d60da592" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Inverses of Exponential Functions With Up to Two Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ee8abd7-c8b0-5395-b1d2-db97d2fbe877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={log}_{7}⁡(x-2)-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537329} {:answer/id #uuid "46f4273e-643e-5140-9bb3-37930495687c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={log}_{7}⁡(x-4)+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537377} {:answer/id #uuid "c58fc5b6-7692-51a1-abef-c6e7a022b6d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{log}_{7}⁡(x-4)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537612} {:answer/id #uuid "d99198fe-8160-5db2-9ef6-8b317c7d5096" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{log}_{7}⁡(x)}{4}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537662} {:answer/id #uuid "e1e17c2b-0b45-5f50-a9cb-32c3aec63676" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={log}_{7}⁡(x-2)+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537683}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46f4273e-643e-5140-9bb3-37930495687c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={log}_{7}⁡(x-4)+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537377} :answer-field/id #uuid "90658ef9-d513-512b-9c60-b9724abb9088" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537818}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a2f709a7-c2aa-55fd-a6a5-4424cd8b1fab" :question/math-academy-id "q-148313" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)={7}^{x-2}+4.$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={7}^{x-2}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={7}^{y-2}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={7}^{y-2}+4 \\\\ x-4 & ={7}^{y-2} \\\\ {log}_{7}⁡(x-4) & ={log}_{7}⁡({7}^{y-2}) \\\\ {log}_{7}⁡(x-4) & =y-2 \\\\ y & ={log}_{7}⁡(x-4)+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)={log}_{7}⁡(x-4)+2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537901 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110701 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfadce43-b527-59fc-b6c7-ec963bb543b4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Inverses of Exponential Functions With More Than Two Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2af70751-5a25-5bbe-a87a-5793529a42b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x+2)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537316} {:answer/id #uuid "45173f49-dca6-5562-aa6b-a16c19825b58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537370} {:answer/id #uuid "5fdb24cb-9d60-58f8-b13e-d7ee93dff0bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x+1)+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537419} {:answer/id #uuid "7045ffc3-7fd2-5590-80b0-9f2bcb29d8e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x-1)-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537447} {:answer/id #uuid "f4779bf5-8fa6-5176-af16-a00529d6cf4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537733}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4779bf5-8fa6-5176-af16-a00529d6cf4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537733} :answer-field/id #uuid "03b4b32e-b15d-5051-934e-0831b4ef34cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537759}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "34bef420-975f-5d0f-9f81-67d4bd446432" :question/math-academy-id "q-148349" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {e}^{x+1}-2.$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {e}^{x+1}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {e}^{y+1}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {e}^{y+1}-2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {e}^{y+1} & =x+2 \\\\ {e}^{y+1} & =2x+4 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{y+1}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4) \\\\ y+1 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4) \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4)-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537979 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110701 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfadce43-b527-59fc-b6c7-ec963bb543b4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Inverses of Exponential Functions With More Than Two Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d1902df-1e1a-57b7-92b9-f031a1d54cb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=2{log}_{3}⁡(x+\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537289} {:answer/id #uuid "b867ae05-4768-52a1-a7ab-9ac578fa106a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={log}_{3}⁡(4x+\\frac{1}{2})+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537587} {:answer/id #uuid "b95fcc5a-b97c-570a-b6aa-9be9f9c37ee4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=2{log}_{3}⁡(4x+\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537589} {:answer/id #uuid "e2312f9d-2baf-5e9d-93dd-0654789452bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(4x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537684} {:answer/id #uuid "e2ef0cbc-d0f2-5d87-96ee-056aed052a7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={log}_{3}⁡(x+\\frac{1}{2})+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537687}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2312f9d-2baf-5e9d-93dd-0654789452bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(4x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537684} :answer-field/id #uuid "52664e87-7542-5e32-b8c8-3ec5d80cbfd4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537783}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e0086fad-533f-5e80-a92f-0ac75b17640c" :question/math-academy-id "q-148359" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {3}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {3}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {3}^{2y}-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {3}^{2y}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {3}^{2y} & =x+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {3}^{2y} & =4x+2 \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡({3}^{2y}) & ={log}_{3}⁡(4x+2) \\\\ 2y & ={log}_{3}⁡(4x+2) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(4x+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(4x+2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537896 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110701 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfadce43-b527-59fc-b6c7-ec963bb543b4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Inverses of Exponential Functions With More Than Two Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11623763-75f4-582c-8cf1-89865dc45e28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537258} {:answer/id #uuid "173a6cfb-d807-5412-8e19-23721709e4c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537277} {:answer/id #uuid "60e2afc2-23df-527e-8d4d-06bcc3f5634f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537420} {:answer/id #uuid "f8d10f1c-8d35-5c14-a45c-90112e2423c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537739} {:answer/id #uuid "f9d7fe5d-6a2b-5e6d-aa5b-be15665a7768" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537744}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "173a6cfb-d807-5412-8e19-23721709e4c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537277} :answer-field/id #uuid "7dd89537-63c4-5254-bd6d-b7533a181887" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537810}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2340627e-913d-5c02-af0a-4af27f0e0758" :question/math-academy-id "q-148367" :question/problem "The function $f(x)=\\frac{2}{3}-{5}^{2x-k}$ has an inverse function given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{2-3x}{3}).\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{2}{3}-{5}^{2x-k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{3}-{5}^{2y-k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{3}-{5}^{2y-k} \\\\ {5}^{2y-k} & =\\frac{2}{3}-x \\\\ {5}^{2y-k} & =\\frac{2-3x}{3} \\\\ {log}_{5}⁡({5}^{2y-k}) & ={log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{2-3x}{3}) \\\\ 2y-k & ={log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{2-3x}{3}) \\\\ 2y & ={log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{2-3x}{3})+k \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{2-3x}{3})+\\frac{k}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{2-3x}{3})+\\frac{k}{2}$  \n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537990 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110701 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfadce43-b527-59fc-b6c7-ec963bb543b4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Inverses of Exponential Functions With More Than Two Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "036eaa53-7479-5120-8bab-05396fca46ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537230} {:answer/id #uuid "56203f17-53a8-5839-bf69-62f170218fff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537406} {:answer/id #uuid "be5dfe54-d2c6-51bc-ba60-5b77415cb27f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537597} {:answer/id #uuid "c7815962-506a-58a7-a401-291b446f0170" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537617} {:answer/id #uuid "ead0b0b9-c829-5ec7-932a-5da8c8741b26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537708}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56203f17-53a8-5839-bf69-62f170218fff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537406} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ce6d141-0aa1-5d3d-a0d1-784268e63420" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537792}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fcede9b9-3aa7-543c-ba0b-831f64ff8671" :question/math-academy-id "q-148370" :question/problem "The function $f(x)=\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {3}^{kx}+2$ has an inverse function given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)={log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}-5).\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {3}^{kx}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {3}^{ky}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {3}^{ky}+2 \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {3}^{ky} & =x-2 \\\\ {3}^{ky} & =\\frac{5x-10}{2} \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡({3}^{ky}) & ={log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}-5) \\\\ ky & ={log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}-5) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{k}{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}-5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{k}{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}-5).$ \n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110985 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110703 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a220ac5f-7b5c-59ef-82d5-aa84654f9fc2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Inverses of Logarithmic Functions With More Than Two Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3dc6496-77c0-520f-b529-b7f00becd583" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4^{\\frac{x}{5}}+1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3dc6496-77c0-520f-b529-b7f00becd583" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4^{\\frac{x}{5}}+1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537646} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7396d89-4bc9-5ba9-b9b1-12012d5351ed" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107628}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9ae8277e-37c6-59e9-af56-0d7eccfa6c61" :question/math-academy-id "q-228842" :question/problem "The inverse of the function $f(x)=5{log}_{4}⁡(10x-1)$ is ${f}^{-1}(x)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5{log}_{4}⁡(10x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5{log}_{4}⁡(10y-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5{log}_{4}⁡(10y-1) \\\\ {log}_{4}⁡(10y-1) & =\\frac{x}{5} \\\\ {4}^{{log}_{4}⁡(10y-1)} & ={4}^{x/5} \\\\ 10y-1 & ={4}^{x/5} \\\\ 10y & ={4}^{x/5}+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{{4}^{x/5}+1}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{4}^{x/5}+1}{10}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537960 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110700 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c01b24c5-c253-5a11-8055-9be0d60da592" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Inverses of Exponential Functions With Up to Two Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d19974ec-20b9-5d83-8c72-b2d36b8377e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\ln(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537638}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d19974ec-20b9-5d83-8c72-b2d36b8377e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\ln(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537638} :answer-field/id #uuid "71520f5c-b440-5a14-bf95-c16d81c6f6d1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537805}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b31f5c4b-dccc-5d6d-b26c-76fe9889b868" :question/math-academy-id "q-250404" :question/problem "The inverse of the function $f(x)={e}^{2-x}$ is ${f}^{-1}(x)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{2-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={e}^{2-y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={e}^{2-y} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2-y}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =2-y \\\\ y & =2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537976 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110700 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c01b24c5-c253-5a11-8055-9be0d60da592" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Inverses of Exponential Functions With Up to Two Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82dfab9f-2880-5450-8652-1147b1151f26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\log_3(-x)}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537481}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82dfab9f-2880-5450-8652-1147b1151f26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\log_3(-x)}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537481} :answer-field/id #uuid "1415ab41-137c-58c7-97a0-9e85dd2a2d52" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d6cea5e8-bf96-5756-ad7c-875f953f91a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-250405" :question/problem "The inverse of the function $f(x)=-{3}^{4x}$ is ${f}^{-1}(x)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-{3}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-{3}^{4y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-{3}^{4y} \\\\ -x & ={3}^{4y} \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡(-x) & ={log}_{3}⁡({3}^{4y}) \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡(-x) & =4y \\\\ 4y & ={log}_{3}⁡(-x) \\\\ y & =\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡(-x)}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡(-x)}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537921 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110700 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c01b24c5-c253-5a11-8055-9be0d60da592" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Inverses of Exponential Functions With Up to Two Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d41088e7-fcca-5795-a840-68f0d4b1a3c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\log_8(3x)}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537647}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d41088e7-fcca-5795-a840-68f0d4b1a3c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\log_8(3x)}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537647} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff656e20-f3df-5084-9cd4-4ad94db5cfe5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "675df8f0-06ab-563c-85f0-2a4a4ed296f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-250406" :question/problem "The inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{{8}^{6x}}{3}$ is ${f}^{-1}(x)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{{8}^{6x}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{8}^{6y}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{8}^{6y}}{3} \\\\ 3x & ={8}^{6y} \\\\ {log}_{8}⁡(3x) & ={log}_{8}⁡({8}^{6y}) \\\\ {log}_{8}⁡(3x) & =6y \\\\ 6y & ={log}_{8}⁡(3x) \\\\ y & =\\frac{{log}_{8}⁡(3x)}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{log}_{8}⁡(3x)}{6}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537942 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110701 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfadce43-b527-59fc-b6c7-ec963bb543b4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Inverses of Exponential Functions With More Than Two Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "750c0d00-afda-5067-a770-e6e2fc508277" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "750c0d00-afda-5067-a770-e6e2fc508277" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537460} :answer-field/id #uuid "7dea4e2d-cd83-5ced-88cf-1d95740dcf47" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537811}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9d666229-2a75-553e-ab1a-3470df672efe" :question/math-academy-id "q-250479" :question/problem "If the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{k}\\cdot {10}^{1+x/2}+1$ has an inverse function given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=2\\operatorname{log}⁡(1-x)-2,\n$$\n\n \nwhat is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{k}\\cdot {10}^{1+x/2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{k}\\cdot {10}^{1+y/2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{k}\\cdot {10}^{1+y/2}+1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{k}\\cdot {10}^{1+y/2} & =x-1 \\\\ {10}^{1+y/2} & =kx-k \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{1+y/2}) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(kx-k) \\\\ 1+\\frac{y}{2} & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(kx-k) \\\\ \\frac{y}{2} & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(kx-k)-1 \\\\ y & =2\\operatorname{log}⁡(kx-k)-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=2\\operatorname{log}⁡(kx-k)-2.$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537986 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110702 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2efb7d6d-25e9-5bf3-b2b3-5085221b0e76" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Inverses of Logarithmic Functions With Up to Two Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef6e90d4-7f02-516b-ac2e-50a86e66d6fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2^{2x-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537721}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef6e90d4-7f02-516b-ac2e-50a86e66d6fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2^{2x-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537721} :answer-field/id #uuid "94613fd4-07f9-5587-8c52-2ee0c031ef37" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537823}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f4092e37-c259-56c3-8698-e5c1a205391f" :question/math-academy-id "q-250483" :question/problem "The inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{2}⁡(x)+\\frac{1}{2}$ is ${f}^{-1}(x)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{2}⁡(x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{2}⁡(y)+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:        \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{2}⁡(y)+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{2}⁡y \\\\ 2x-1 & ={log}_{2}⁡y \\\\ {2}^{2x-1} & ={2}^{{log}_{2}⁡y} \\\\ {2}^{2x-1} & =y \\\\ y & ={2}^{2x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)={2}^{2x-1}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537882 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110703 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a220ac5f-7b5c-59ef-82d5-aa84654f9fc2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Inverses of Logarithmic Functions With More Than Two Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4655caa9-d5c5-51c5-8bd4-8475855ddad8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4({3}^{4x-4})+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537372}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4655caa9-d5c5-51c5-8bd4-8475855ddad8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4({3}^{4x-4})+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537372} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2d74a14-bc3d-5d29-8730-61d8c4d650f9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537859}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "04376890-0a44-56a8-b428-8110db81b255" :question/math-academy-id "q-250505" :question/problem "The inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{4}{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{1}{2})+1$ is ${f}^{-1}(x)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{4}{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{1}{2})+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{y}{4}-\\frac{1}{2})+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{y}{4}-\\frac{1}{2})+1 \\\\ x-1 & =\\frac{1}{4}{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{y}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ 4x-4 & ={log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{y}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{y}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}) & =4x-4 \\\\ {3}^{{log}_{3}⁡(y/4-1/2)} & ={3}^{4x-4} \\\\ \\frac{y}{4}-\\frac{1}{2} & ={3}^{4x-4} \\\\ \\frac{y}{4} & ={3}^{4x-4}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y & =4({3}^{4x-4}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ y & =4({3}^{4x-4})+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=4({3}^{4x-4})+2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537905 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110703 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a220ac5f-7b5c-59ef-82d5-aa84654f9fc2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Inverses of Logarithmic Functions With More Than Two Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f374302c-dbd0-5652-afd7-79c66b75d75d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537730}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f374302c-dbd0-5652-afd7-79c66b75d75d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537730} :answer-field/id #uuid "075d7fee-b2d5-5957-a28d-a7eec528b93a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3b5ffb6f-7067-559d-aec0-81e9ef0a06b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-250507" :question/problem "If the function $f(x)={log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{x-1}{5})-k$ has an inverse function given by\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=5\\cdot {2}^{x-3}+1,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{x-1}{5})-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{y-1}{5})-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{y-1}{5})-k \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{y-1}{5}) & =x+k \\\\ {2}^{{log}_{2}⁡((y-1)/5)} & ={2}^{x+k} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{5} & ={2}^{x+k} \\\\ y-1 & =5\\cdot {2}^{x+k} \\\\ y & =5\\cdot {2}^{x+k}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=5\\cdot {2}^{x+k}+1.$ \n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537889 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110703 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a220ac5f-7b5c-59ef-82d5-aa84654f9fc2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Inverses of Logarithmic Functions With More Than Two Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2500f223-2c89-5c0f-a8ea-dd1a400d8458" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537308}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2500f223-2c89-5c0f-a8ea-dd1a400d8458" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537308} :answer-field/id #uuid "08ffe6b8-fc77-5a91-a8ee-c6dda37b1ac2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "199584af-715c-57d8-b77d-cfea8258f652" :question/math-academy-id "q-250510" :question/problem "If the function $f(x)=3{log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{x-1}{3})-k?$ has an inverse function given by\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=3\\cdot {2}^{(x+6)/3}+1,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=3{log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{x-1}{3})-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=3{log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{y-1}{3})-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3{log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{y-1}{3})-k \\\\ 3{log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{y-1}{3}) & =x+k \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{y-1}{3}) & =\\frac{x+k}{3} \\\\ {2}^{{log}_{2}⁡((y-1)/3)} & ={2}^{(x+k)/3} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{3} & ={2}^{(x+k)/3} \\\\ y-1 & =3\\cdot {2}^{(x+k)/3} \\\\ y & =3\\cdot {2}^{(x+k)/3}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=3\\cdot {2}^{(x+k)/3}+1.$ \n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537940 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110702 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2efb7d6d-25e9-5bf3-b2b3-5085221b0e76" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Inverses of Logarithmic Functions With Up to Two Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20e1836d-75eb-5de8-9c9c-af521731af67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{10}^{x}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537301} {:answer/id #uuid "338596eb-3c1b-5bb8-a994-1d2cbd79a6de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={10}^{x/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537338} {:answer/id #uuid "a68559e7-d056-5e43-81d2-70700c2a99e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=3\\cdot {10}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537543} {:answer/id #uuid "b4ad9448-aed0-5988-be94-5a5e58fa5d2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={10}^{x}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537578} {:answer/id #uuid "cdffd6c4-c3d8-5c8f-9f1a-ca2d437d5ed7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={10}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537631}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a68559e7-d056-5e43-81d2-70700c2a99e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=3\\cdot {10}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537543} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ff5f77d-c752-52bb-b6ec-a317055ea25e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537770}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9b1ff915-f7df-590e-9573-48667059c317" :question/math-academy-id "q-46621" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}).$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{y}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{y}{3}) \\\\ {10}^{x} & ={10}^{\\operatorname{log}⁡(y/3)} \\\\ {10}^{x} & =\\frac{y}{3} \\\\ y & =3\\cdot {10}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=3\\cdot {10}^{x}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537964 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110702 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2efb7d6d-25e9-5bf3-b2b3-5085221b0e76" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Inverses of Logarithmic Functions With Up to Two Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "196682b1-bbf3-576e-ba6f-a649f7fc16ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={2}^{x}-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537282} {:answer/id #uuid "40d40771-4eff-5689-a13b-0ce325f7611e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={2}^{x-8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537364} {:answer/id #uuid "7d88a917-1a9a-5eac-a57a-00685fc9b5ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={2}^{8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537474} {:answer/id #uuid "c58c52c9-7d88-54cc-b33c-6e8f43208112" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={2}^{x+8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537611} {:answer/id #uuid "d73af309-c34a-5c48-b101-ba26f74e0435" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={2}^{x}+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40d40771-4eff-5689-a13b-0ce325f7611e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={2}^{x-8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537364} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8174fdc-5c9a-5c07-a9f4-f2a0413bcb78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bc418213-398e-54af-8583-a0bcd69d268b" :question/math-academy-id "q-46622" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)={log}_{2}⁡(x)+8.$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={log}_{2}⁡(x)+8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={log}_{2}⁡(y)+8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equations, as follows:        \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={log}_{2}⁡(y)+8 \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(y) & =x-8 \\\\ {2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(y)} & ={2}^{x-8} \\\\ y & ={2}^{x-8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)={2}^{x-8}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537965 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110702 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2efb7d6d-25e9-5bf3-b2b3-5085221b0e76" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Inverses of Logarithmic Functions With Up to Two Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "448a3c24-8cfd-569c-b0a3-1c765de60d34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={e}^{x/7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537369} {:answer/id #uuid "4924d51b-e261-507e-adfc-8135105e1bc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=7{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537384} {:answer/id #uuid "5fd2814c-01a6-54bb-ace6-f4371712810f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={e}^{x}+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537418} {:answer/id #uuid "a9d3cc1a-fcfb-5bbd-b5a0-9fde202d0224" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={e}^{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537549} {:answer/id #uuid "acc3787f-1577-51a3-8e8c-ba211e4d0309" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537555}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "acc3787f-1577-51a3-8e8c-ba211e4d0309" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537555} :answer-field/id #uuid "c659a6ca-0f92-5137-b8eb-7f163fce4f16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537853}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bf8490ce-55f6-59c1-8771-a458611eb22b" :question/math-academy-id "q-46629" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7x).$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:        \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7y) \\\\ {e}^{x} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7y)} \\\\ {e}^{x} & =7y \\\\ y & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{7}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537919 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110702 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2efb7d6d-25e9-5bf3-b2b3-5085221b0e76" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Inverses of Logarithmic Functions With Up to Two Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08846a6c-13c1-5b18-885e-0b1adeacc3f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537239} {:answer/id #uuid "23b61276-d5bf-535a-a27d-e9a54b1b9dbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537306} {:answer/id #uuid "395537a6-7a30-5d62-853c-cb1f3b891411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537347} {:answer/id #uuid "3d74ec5f-ab18-5937-82d1-2c8a347321a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537356} {:answer/id #uuid "54b483d6-0bec-5ca5-9f74-c3fcf0ede81f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537405}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d74ec5f-ab18-5937-82d1-2c8a347321a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537356} :answer-field/id #uuid "a19c6a94-00d3-5ae9-abde-806f2ebd3f92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537834}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6267302e-96c5-5885-be5f-0252c2b87de1" :question/math-academy-id "q-81645" :question/problem "The function $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x-k)$ has an inverse function given by ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}-9}{3}.$ What is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x-k)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3y-k).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3y-k) \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3y-k)} & ={e}^{x} \\\\ 3y-k & ={e}^{x} \\\\ 3y & ={e}^{x}+k \\\\ y & =\\frac{{e}^{x}+k}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}+k}{3}.$ \n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=-9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537880 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110703 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a220ac5f-7b5c-59ef-82d5-aa84654f9fc2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Inverses of Logarithmic Functions With More Than Two Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "161bbede-61ba-5590-8f41-6ce72d807195" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} 